 would ever happen no one was going  to look into anything  so well they've made public statements  both law enforcement and the prosecutor  have made public statements that it's a  cold case  um and that it's closed but  we know it's anything but should be  anything but closed  and we want to fight for the people that  have done this to be held responsible  and we want someone to truly investigate  you know everything that was never  investigated  and those people still walk out there  today  and let me tell you that that is deeply  sickening  it's pretty scary actually and it's  scary i don't know  i don't i don't know what could be done  or what can happen  you know it you know we know it's old  you know we know it's  cold but you know what we need someone  to do their job  for surely so you really want a chance  for another trial  absolutely i'm all for it let's go back  and really show people how things  were uh show them the true facts  not the smoke and mirrors that came in  the civil suit  so yep and then  i'm gung-ho i'm ready  all right thank you both very very much  for coming in and talking with us  thank you thank you brian  up next we'll hear from the other side  from bill carter jason carter's father  you're watching brian ross investigates  on the long crime  trial network  we continue now with our report on the  family tragedy involving the carter  family of iowa and we're joined by mark  weinhardt who's the lawyer for bill  carter mr weinhardt thank you for being  here you'll argue before the court this  week  about the case whether it should be  overturned or there should be a new  trial  why do you think he should not get a new  trial  well brian uh i'm going to be arguing to  the supreme court in 48 hours  and i don't think it's appropriate for  me to preview my  argument to the justices uh publicly i  will say  that we have every confidence that the  judge who conducted the civil trial in  december of 2017  did it right she made the right  decisions about what evidence did and  did come  into the case uh the jury heard that  evidence they made a decision very  quickly in just two hours  uh determined that jason was liable for  killing his mother  uh we think that that was the right  decision and  uh on thursday afternoon we're going to  defend that decision in the iowa supreme  court  as you know the argument is that there's  new information about other possible  suspects who were never  according to jason carter's lawyers  fully investigated  does bill carter think still that his  son  killed shirley carter bill is  firmly convinced that jason killed  shirley  which has to be one of the most shocking  and troubling things that a father could  ever conclude  about his own offspring and yet bill  came to that conclusion based on the  evidence  he is firm in that conclusion the  so-called  new evidence is something that jason had  an  opportunity to present to the judge who  heard  the civil trial he spent three days we  had a second trial effectively  for three days where he was able to  present all of that evidence to that  judge  that judge decided two things first she  decided that that evidence which was  rumors hearsay innuendo from people in  jail trying to make  deals because of their own criminal  circumstances uh she decided that that  evidence  was not admissible that even if there  were a new trial the jury wouldn't  be allowed to hear it because under the  rules of evidence it simply did not come  into the case  second she decided that that evidence  simply did not square  with the uncontested facts of the case  that just did not match the physical  facts  of how it was that shirley was murdered  and so she determined that that evidence  did not warrant a new trial and that's  what we're going to be defending in two  days is the judge's decision  about how that alleged evidence does not  in fact change the case at all  mr weinhardt a question for you from  rhonda schwartz rhonda  it's such a family tragedy how is your  client bill carter who's in his 80s  holding up through all this  you know um bill is a man of routine  and he's a man of the earth he has been  farming  the same plot of land for more than 50  years and he's grown that farm he's a  scrimper and a saver  uh and uh the rhythm  of the planting season and  the rhythm of life in the farm economy  that's what keeps him going it's what  keeps him uh putting one foot in front  of the other  every day um were he to retire i don't  know what would happen to him but as  long as he's got the farm and as long as  he's got that life  uh in the very home where he and surely  spent all those decades  on the land that the two of them work  together as long as he's got that  uh he gets by sadder  wiser but he gets by  all right mark weinhardt thank you so  much we look forward to  watching your arguments before the iowa  supreme court and continue to report on  the story  a program reminder a story about the  carter case  will appear on a e thursday night at 10  as part of the lawn  crime production series killer cases i'm  brian ross thank you for joining us  and thanks to everyone at the law and  crime team who so ably get us  on the air and off the air without a  hitch  [Music]  so
 

